<description>
The `onig_scan` function scans a string for a regular expression. It takes a compiled regular expression, a string to search, a region to store match results, and a callback function to be executed for each match. The function returns the number of matches found, or an error code if something went wrong.
</description>

<requirements>

<requirement>
The `reg` argument must be a valid `regex_t` object created by `onig_new`. This is because the function internally accesses members of this struct, so a NULL or invalid pointer will cause a crash.
</requirement>
<requirement>
The `str` and `end` arguments define the memory range to be scanned. `end` must be greater than or equal to `str`.
</requirement>
<requirement>
The `region` argument must be a valid `OnigRegion` object created with `onig_region_new()`. This is because the function writes match data to this object.
</requirement>
<requirement>
The `scan_callback` argument must be a valid function pointer. This function is called for each match found during the scan.
</requirement>
<requirement>
Before calling `onig_scan` or any other oniguruma functions, `onig_initialize()` must be called to set up the library and encoding information.
</requirement>
<requirement>
After the scan is complete and the `regex_t` object is no longer needed, `onig_free()` should be called to release its memory.
</requirement>
<requirement>
After all oniguruma operations are finished, `onig_end()` should be called for final cleanup.
</requirement>

</requirements>

</function-requirements>

