<crash-analysis>
**Analysis:**
The crash is a heap-buffer-overflow read, confirmed by both the AddressSanitizer report and GDB analysis. The root cause is a bug in the `left_adjust_char_head` function within the Oniguruma library (`/src/jq/vendor/oniguruma/src/utf8.c`).

The function is called by `match_at` and is passed a pointer `s` which can, under certain conditions, point to the byte immediately following the end of the string buffer. The function's code is:
```c
static OnigUChar*
left_adjust_char_head(const OnigUChar* start, const OnigUChar* s)
{
const OnigUChar *p;

if (s <= start) return (OnigUChar* )s;
p = s;

while (!UTF8_IS_LEAD(*p) && p > start) { // Crash on *p
p--;
}
return (OnigUChar* )p;
}
```
When `s` is the end-of-buffer pointer, `p` is initialized to this address. The `while` loop condition `!UTF8_IS_LEAD(*p)` attempts to dereference `p` *before* the boundary check `p > start` is evaluated. This results in reading one byte out of bounds, causing the crash.

The fuzz driver is not at fault; it correctly uses the `onig_scan` API. The bug lies within the internal implementation of the Oniguruma library, which fails to handle this edge case safely.

**Suggestion:**
The `left_adjust_char_head` function in `utf8.c` must be patched to prevent the out-of-bounds read. The pointer boundary must be checked before any dereferencing occurs.

A safe implementation would restructure the loop to ensure `p` is within the `[start, s)` range before being dereferenced.

**Recommended Patch:**
```c
// In /src/jq/vendor/oniguruma/src/utf8.c

static OnigUChar*
left_adjust_char_head(const OnigUChar* start, const OnigUChar* s)
{
const OnigUChar *p = s;

if (p <= start) return (OnigUChar*)p;

// Start search from the character before p
p--; 

while (p > start) {
if (UTF8_IS_LEAD(*p)) {
return (OnigUChar*)p;
}
p--;
}

// Check the first character
if (UTF8_IS_LEAD(*p)) {
return (OnigUChar*)p;
}

// As a fallback, if no lead byte is found (e.g., malformed string),
// the original function's behavior was to return the adjusted pointer.
// Returning start seems like a safe default in this ambiguous case.
return (OnigUChar*)start;
}
```
This revised logic correctly handles the case where `s` is an end-of-buffer pointer by starting the search from `s-1`, thus staying within the valid memory region.
</crash-analysis>

